<h2>How Steam Cleaning Works on Interior Surfaces</h2>
<p>Commercial steam machines heat water beyond boiling point and release vapour through controlled nozzles. At the point of contact, steam temperature kills most common bacteria and dust mites while loosening grime from fibres and hard surfaces. Because moisture content is low compared to traditional extraction shampooing, seats and carpets dry within hours rather than days—a significant advantage in Punjab's humidity where damp interiors quickly develop musty smells.</p>
<h3>Dashboard and Plastics</h3>
<p>Heat rejuvenates dull plastics slightly while removing sticky residue from hands and air pollution. Technicians use brushes wrapped in microfibre to agitate without scratching soft-touch materials common in modern cabins.</p>
<h3>Fabric and Alcantara</h3>
<p>Steam lifts stains from seat fibres when paired with extraction equipment. Alcantara and suede-like materials particularly benefit because excessive wetting damages their texture permanently.</p>
<h3>Leather Upholstery</h3>
<p>Leather responds well to gentle steam followed immediately by conditioning. The heat opens pores slightly, allowing cleaners to penetrate, while subsequent conditioner application prevents drying and cracking.</p>

