<h2>Two Technologies, Different Purposes</h2>
<p>Steam wash and pressure wash are often discussed as competitors, but they serve fundamentally different roles in automotive cleaning. Pressure washing uses high-volume water at elevated pressure to blast away heavy contamination. Steam washing uses superheated low-moisture vapour to dissolve and lift grime with minimal mechanical force. Choosing correctly—or combining both strategically—determines whether you clean effectively or damage paint in the process.</p>

<h2>Pressure Washing: Strengths and Risks</h2>
<h3>Where Pressure Excels</h3>
<p>Underbody mud removal after off-road driving or flood navigation. Wheel arch cleaning where heavy packed dirt requires force. Concrete driveways and garage floors—not paint. At safe distances with wide fan tips, pressure rinse before hand washing can dislodge loose debris efficiently.</p>
<h3>Where Pressure Fails</h3>
<p>Close-range pressure on paint strips clear coat. Water forced into door seals, mirror housings, and trim gaps causes electrical problems. Cheap pressure washers with narrow jets act like liquid sandpaper on Punjab's dust-coated panels.</p>

<h2>Steam Washing: Strengths and Limits</h2>
<h3>Where Steam Excels</h3>
<p>Paint-safe exterior cleaning with minimal water. Interior sanitisation without soaking. Engine bay degreasing with controlled moisture. Door jamb and crevice detailing. Removing light to moderate road film without micro-scratching.</p>
<h3>Where Steam Struggles</h3>
<p>Extremely heavy dried mud caked on underbody may need pre-rinse loosening. Very large commercial vehicles may require combined approaches due to surface area and time constraints.</p>

<h2>Side-by-Side Comparison</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Paint safety:</strong> Steam wins decisively for body panels</li>
<li><strong>Water consumption:</strong> Steam uses 5–15 litres; pressure uses 150–300+ litres</li>
<li><strong>Heavy mud removal:</strong> Pressure wins on underbody with proper technique</li>
<li><strong>Interior cleaning:</strong> Steam only—pressure is destructive inside cabins</li>
<li><strong>Sanitisation:</strong> Steam kills bacteria; pressure does not</li>
<li><strong>Environmental impact:</strong> Steam significantly lower runoff and consumption</li>
</ul>

