<h2>Why Punjab Eats Alloy Wheels</h2>
<p>Alloy wheels face attack from every direction: brake dust corrosion, road salt near the coast, curb damage exposing bare aluminium, and monsoon humidity that keeps wheels perpetually damp. Within three to four years, neglected alloys develop pitting, clear coat peeling, and whitish oxidation around lug nuts that no amount of scrubbing reverses. Prevention costs far less than refurbishment or replacement.</p>

<h2>Types of Alloy Finishes</h2>
<h3>Clear-Coated Painted Alloys</h3>
<p>Most factory wheels have paint and clear coat layers. Brake dust penetrates damaged clear coat, causing bubbling and peeling. Regular iron remover treatment prevents embedded contamination.</p>
<h3>Diamond-Cut / Machined Alloys</h3>
<p>Polished metal faces with lacquer protection are extremely vulnerable—curb contact removes lacquer instantly, exposing metal to corrosion. Avoid kerb contact and consider PPF on diamond-cut faces.</p>
<h3>Bare Polished Aluminium</h3>
<p>Aftermarket and some performance wheels lack protective coating entirely. Require sealant or ceramic coating service application and frequent maintenance.</p>

<h2>Prevention Protocol</h2>
<ul>
<li>Clean wheels fortnightly minimum—weekly during monsoon</li>
<li>Always use iron fallout remover before agitation</li>
<li>Never use acid-based wheel cleaners on coated alloys</li>
<li>Dry wheels completely after washing to prevent water spot etching</li>
<li>Apply wheel-specific ceramic coating annually</li>
<li>Repair curb damage and touch up chips immediately</li>
<li>Torque lug nuts correctly—overtightening cracks clear coat</li>
